1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The 17th Century Polish Nobility http://www.almanachdegotha.org/id221.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Łukasz Opaliński; Coat of arms	Clan Łodzia; Consort	Izabela Tęczyńska (1639); Noble family	Opaliński; Father	Piotr Opaliński; Mother	Zofia Kostka; Born	1612; Sieraków; Died	June 15, 1666 (aged 53–54); Rytwiany;
2 CONT
2 CONT Łukasz de Bnin Opaliński of Clan Łodzia (1612–1666[1]) was a Polish nobleman. He was a poet, writer and political activist, one of the most important of Polish political writers of the 17th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was Lord Starost Pobiedziński since 1631, Podkomorzy of Kalisz 1638-1640 and Podkomorzy of Poznań since 1640. He was Court Marshal of the Crown since 1650.[2] He served as Marshal of the regular Sejm from March 10 to May 1, 163
2 CONC 8 in Warsaw.[2] He was the brother of Krzysztof Opaliński.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was educated at the Lubrański Academy in Poznań, and also at the University of Leuven, the University of Orléans, the University of Strasbourg and the University of Padua.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage with Izabela Tęczyńska in 1639 allowed him to inherit the significant estates of the Tęczyński family. Bibliophile himself, just like his brother, he expanded the library inherited form Izabela's uncle, Jan Tęczyński. His
2 CONC  library was reckoned to be one of the largest private libraries in Europe and he had contacts with most of the printers in Europe, often receiving the first editions of many important works.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had three children with his wife: Jan Opaliński, Stanisław Opaliński and Zofia Opalińska.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the age of 20 during the Election Sejm of 1632, he voted for king Władysław IV Waza. Active politician, participant of many Sejms and Sejm commissions (military, treasury, diplomacy). He advocated criticized the shortcomings of
2 CONC  the Golden Liberty[2] and demanded political reforms, among them the reformation of Sejm deliberation procedures (in his Rozmowa Plebana z Ziemianinem... (Conversations of a Parson with a Country Squire), 1641),[2] opposed liberu
2 CONC m veto and abuse of power by magnates (in his Coś nowego..., 1652). After the death of Władysław IV he supported the election of his brother, Jan Kazimierz and supported him during the Swedish invasion The Deluge, even after his b
2 CONC rother Krzysztof Opaliński defected to the Swedes in 1655. Supported the vivente rege proposals at the Sejms of 1661 and 1662.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1652 he was the chairman of the Sejm Tribunalkl which banished Hieronim Radziejowski for collaboration with the Swedes during The Deluge.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1661 he was a cofounder of the first Polish newspaper, the Merkuriusz Polski.[2] That year he published Poeta nowy (New Poet), the first work in Polish on the principles of poetry.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT He is also credited with the creation of the soccer variant woodske.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in Rytwiany.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C5%81ukasz_Opali%C5%84ski_(1612%E2%80%931666)
